sections.s2_technical:
The status code must reflect the failure category. Bad input is 400, authentication failure is 401, permission failure is 403, missing resource is 404, a state conflict like a duplicate is 409, a business-rule failure on valid input is 422, and an unexpected server failure is 500. This is non-negotiable because the status is the one field the whole ecosystem reads.

Returning 200 with success false breaks three systems at once. Clients: HTTP libraries and generated API clients treat 2xx as success and will not trigger their error paths, so the application silently mishandles failures. Monitoring: dashboards count HTTP status, so a wall of fake 200s hides a real outage and SLO alerts never fire. Retry logic: proxies and clients decide to retry based on status, so a 200 is never retried even if the real failure was a transient 503, while a real 400 would be retried forever if also disguised. Disguising failures as 200 defeats the purpose of HTTP.

A good error body is consistent across every endpoint so clients write one parser. It separates a stable machine-readable code from a human-readable message. The code, like INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS, never changes wording, so client logic can switch on it reliably, while the message, like your balance is too low, can be reworded or localised freely. Changing the message must never break a client, which is why the two are separate.

```java
// A consistent, machine-friendly error body.
public record ApiError(
    String code,        // STABLE: e.g. "INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS" — clients switch on this
    String message,     // human-readable, may change/localise
    String traceId,     // correlation id to find logs
    Instant timestamp) {}
```

The body must include a trace or correlation id that also appears in the server logs, so support can take the id from the customer and find the exact request. It must never include a stack trace or internal details, because those leak implementation and class names, help attackers, and confuse clients. Stack traces go to the logs, not the response.

Centralising error handling with RestControllerAdvice is essential. Instead of try-catch in every controller, which drifts into inconsistency, one advice class maps each exception type to a status and a body. This guarantees every endpoint returns the same shape and the same status rules.

```java
@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {
    @ExceptionHandler(InsufficientFundsException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiError> handle(InsufficientFundsException e) {
        String traceId = MDC.get("traceId");
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY)
            .body(new ApiError("INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS", e.getMessage(), traceId, Instant.now()));
    }
}
```

A senior nuance: the stable code is part of your API contract just like the URLs. Once clients switch on INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS, you can never rename it without breaking them, so choose codes carefully and document them. The human message is free to change. This separation is what lets you improve user-facing wording for years without a single client-breaking change.

